 "result_text": "I changed `Light.set_light` in `src/uiprotect/data/devices.py` to fix controlling a floodlight that Protect has already turned on. I haven't run any tests or tried it against a real device.\n\n**Cause (my diagnosis from the code, not reproduced):** `set_light` only sets `is_led_force_on`. When Protect itself turns the light on (for example on motion), `is_light_on` is true but the force flag is still false. Asking to turn it off then changes nothing, so no update is sent to the controller. The same thing happens in the other direction if the flag is already true and the light is off.\n\n**Fix:** `set_light` now checks whether the force flag already matches the requested state while the light's actual state differs. If so, it first sends a toggle to the opposite value, then sends the real change.\n\nThis means that case now sends two updates instead of one. A single update would be cleaner if the controller accepts one, but I couldn't check that without a device.",
